Draw desktop icons the way Windows 11 does.

A selected item gets a translucent light-blue box around the whole of it --
icon and label together -- and an item under the pointer a fainter one,
instead of a solid box in the theme's selection colour behind the label
alone.  The label stays white over its shadow in every state, and the shadow
is soft (two offset copies at falling opacity) rather than a hard one-pixel
copy.  Keyboard focus on an unselected item is a thin light frame instead of
the theme's focus ring, which is meant for widgets on a window background and
sits heavily on a wallpaper; like Windows, it shows only once the keyboard
has been used to move around.  Leaving the desktop clears the hover box.

Everything is drawn inside the item's own rectangle (icon and label
together).  That rectangle is what redraw_item() repaints when an item's
state changes and what the expose handler tests an item against, so a box
drawn even a pixel outside it was never erased: hovering or selecting an
icon left a thin outline on the wallpaper.

